  "worked_solution": "Notice that the graph of $y=-f(2x)+3$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis,\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis, and finally\n\n- translating it by $3$ units up.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global **minimum** of $y=f(x)$ to the global **maximum** of $y=-f(2x)+3.$ So, we need to find the image of $(2,3),$ the global minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-f(2x)+3,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(2,3).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph  along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ and get the graph of $y=f(2x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2,3)=(1,3).\n$$\n\n- Next, we flip the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(2x).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,-1\\cdot 3)=(1,-3).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we translate the result by $3$ units up and get the graph of $y=-f(2x)+3.$ After this transformation, the point $(1,-3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,-3+3)=(1,0).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global maximum of $y=-f(2x)+3$ is $(1,0).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
